From: Mateusz Krawczuk <mateusz.krawczuk@cybrixsystems.com>
Subject: [PATCH] memory: tegra: tegra186-emc: honor ICC requests as an EMC clock floor on T186/T194

tegra186-emc registers an interconnect provider for Tegra186/194/234,
but its .set callback (tegra_emc_icc_set_bw) is an empty stub: its
comment says bandwidth is "passed to BPMP-FW in the BW set function of
the MC driver".  That is only true on Tegra234, whose
tegra234_mc_icc_set() sends MRQ_BWMGR_INT to the BPMP.  Tegra194 and
Tegra186 use the generic tegra_mc_icc_ops from mc.c whose .set is ALSO
a no-op stub.

Net effect on T194: every icc_set_bw() from every client is silently
discarded and the EMC stays at whatever conservative frequency the
boot chain chose.  This starves latency-sensitive DMA clients (the
on-board EQOS ethernet overflows its RX FIFO and stalls under
sustained load at default clocks; maxing the EMC with jetson_clocks
makes the symptom vanish).

Implement the EMC provider .set for chips whose BPMP firmware does not
support MRQ_BWMGR_INT (mc->bwmgr_mrq_supported == false, i.e. T186 and
T194): translate the aggregated bandwidth on the EMEM node into an EMC
clock floor via clk_set_min_rate(), following the pattern of
tegra124-emc's emc_icc_set().  On these chips the EMC clock is a BPMP
clock, so the rate request reaches the firmware - the debugfs min_rate
file in this very driver already relies on exactly that.

The bandwidth-to-rate divisor is the number of DRAM bytes moved per
EMC clock cycle: 64 on 256-bit parts (AGX Xavier), 32 on 128-bit parts
(Xavier NX).  Use 32, since the BPMP does not report the bus width
here; on a 256-bit part the computed floor is conservative by at most
2x, and it is only a floor - the BPMP still arbitrates upward for
other clients and the request is dropped when consumers release it.

Behaviour on T234 (bwmgr_mrq_supported) is unchanged: return 0 and let
the MC driver's BPMP path handle it.

Upstream-Status: Pending (mainline candidate; needs discussion on the
bytes-per-cycle constant vs. querying the BPMP for the DRAM config)
Signed-off-by: Mateusz Krawczuk <mateusz.krawczuk@cybrixsystems.com>
---
--- a/drivers/memory/tegra/tegra186-emc.c
+++ b/drivers/memory/tegra/tegra186-emc.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/interconnect-provider.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
@@ -218,16 +219,45 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Minimum number of DRAM bytes transferred per EMC clock cycle across
+ * supported Tegra186/Tegra194 memory configurations: 128-bit LPDDR4(X)
+ * at double data rate (Jetson Xavier NX).  256-bit parts (Jetson AGX
+ * Xavier, Jetson TX2) move 64 bytes/cycle, so for them the computed
+ * floor is conservative by at most 2x.
+ */
+#define TEGRA186_EMC_MIN_BYTES_PER_CYCLE	32
+
+/*
  * tegra_emc_icc_set_bw() - Set BW api for EMC provider
  * @src: ICC node for External Memory Controller (EMC)
  * @dst: ICC node for External Memory (DRAM)
  *
- * Do nothing here as info to BPMP-FW is now passed in the BW set function
- * of the MC driver. BPMP-FW sets the final Freq based on the passed values.
+ * On Tegra234 the bandwidth info is passed to the BPMP-FW via
+ * MRQ_BWMGR_INT in the MC driver's BW set function and the BPMP-FW sets
+ * the final frequency, so nothing needs to be done here.
+ *
+ * On Tegra186/Tegra194 the BPMP-FW does not implement MRQ_BWMGR_INT and
+ * the MC driver's set callback is a no-op, so translate the aggregated
+ * bandwidth into an EMC clock floor here.  The EMC clock is a BPMP
+ * clock, therefore clk_set_min_rate() reaches the firmware.
  */
 static int tegra_emc_icc_set_bw(struct icc_node *src, struct icc_node *dst)
 {
-	return 0;
+	struct tegra186_emc *emc = container_of(dst->provider,
+						struct tegra186_emc, provider);
+	struct tegra_mc *mc = dev_get_drvdata(emc->dev->parent);
+	unsigned long long rate;
+
+	/* BPMP-FW manages the frequency itself (Tegra234 and later) */
+	if (mc->bwmgr_mrq_supported)
+		return 0;
+
+	rate = max(icc_units_to_bps(dst->avg_bw),
+		   icc_units_to_bps(dst->peak_bw));
+	do_div(rate, TEGRA186_EMC_MIN_BYTES_PER_CYCLE);
+	rate = min_t(u64, rate, U32_MAX);
+
+	return clk_set_min_rate(emc->clk, rate);
 }
 
 static struct icc_node *
